The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```text
down_write(&leds_list_lock);
list_add_tail(&led_cdev->node, &leds_list);
up_write(&leds_list_lock);
```
Reason:
These three lines were placed before `led_init_core(led_cdev);`. This caused the LED to be added to the global `leds_list` before `set_brightness_work` (initialized inside `led_init_core`) was ready. A default trigger’s registration could then access the LED via `leds_list` and call `led_set_brightness()`, which queues the uninitialized work, leading to a kernel warning or crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```text
down_write(&leds_list_lock);
list_add_tail(&led_cdev->node, &leds_list);
up_write(&leds_list_lock);
```
Reason:
These three lines are now placed **after** `led_init_core(led_cdev);`. This ensures the LED is only added to the global list after `set_brightness_work` and other core fields have been initialized, closing the race window where a default trigger could schedule work on an uninitialized workqueue.

